by wachterjohannes at 2026-08-23T19:36:16Z

Answering the three parts separately.

**Why tools, resources and resource templates.** Tools carry an input schema generated from the signature plus `@param`. That is what `tools:inspect` prints, and the same reflection drives the argument casting, so a wrong type fails with a message instead of reaching your code. A skill pointing at a bare command has none of that. Resources exist for a different reason, navigation: `symfony-profiler://profile/{token}` returns the collectors that profile actually has, and only then do you read one of them. Without that shape you either push a whole profile into the context window or invent one command per collector.

**Why not adopt `bin/console debug:*`.** Mate never boots the kernel, it parses the compiled `*DebugContainer.xml` and reads the profiler storage from disk. So it still answers when the application does not boot, which is exactly the situation you are debugging in. And the output is shaped for an agent (summary vs data, duplicate queries grouped, secrets redacted, `--format=json|toon`) where `debug:*` prints tables for humans.

**On models calling debug commands on their own.** Not just anecdotal, I see it too. The materialized agent instructions and the skills steer it toward Mate instead, and that part works really well in practice. Bringing the two together stays open: Mate could wrap the debug commands, or the debug commands could point at Mate when it is installed. Both are worth looking at later.

You are right that this rationale is missing from the docs. It goes into the docs pass.

